Content-Type: text/plain; charset="UTF-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 7bit

Thanks for the report. We're closing this as hardening, not a vulnerability. The secret is only compared after a database lookup on the key's 16-character identifier, which is mostly random and isn't exposed to anyone but the key's owner, so an attacker has no way to target a key's secret without already holding most of the token. On top of that, the difference in a 32-byte string compare is nanoseconds, well below the noise of a network round trip through PHP and a database query, and the report includes no measurement showing otherwise. We agree hash_equals() is the better choice there, so feel free to open a regular pull request against main with that change.

-- 
